<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">30 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 30 March, from 1282 to 1981.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1282 CE <span class="tih-ago">744 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sicilian Vespers uprising begins in Palermo</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At the hour of vespers outside the church of Santo Spirito, Sicilians rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>'s French rule, killing thousands. The island passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1814 CE <span class="tih-ago">212 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Allied armies win the Battle of Paris</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Russian, Prussian and Austrian forces stormed the heights around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the city capitulated that night. Coalition troops entered the next day, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> abdicated within a week.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1842 CE <span class="tih-ago">184 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Crawford Long uses ether to anaesthetise a patient</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In Jefferson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, Dr Crawford Long removed a tumour from James Venable's neck after giving him ether. He did not publish until 1849, after William Morton's public demonstration.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1856 CE <span class="tih-ago">170 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Paris ends the Crimean War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> accepted the neutralisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, barring warships from its waters, and gave up southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. The powers guaranteed the integrity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1867 CE <span class="tih-ago">159 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">United States agrees to buy Alaska from Russia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">William Seward signed a treaty to buy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> for 7.2 million dollars, about two cents an acre. Mocked as 'Seward's Folly', it proved rich in gold, fish and oil.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1981 CE <span class="tih-ago">45 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ronald Reagan shot outside the Washington Hilton</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">John Hinckley Jr. wounded the President and three others, including press secretary James Brady. Reagan, hit in the chest, recovered quickly, and his humour in hospital boosted his popularity.</p></div></li></ol>
